;;; A rule's false `neq` conclusion is stored as a fact —
;;; regression `assert-neq-x-x` (`corpus/regressions.toml`).
;;;
;;; ── What this program is ───────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; One fact `(p a)` and a rule concluding `(neq ?x ?x)` from `(p ?x)`.
;;;
;;; ── The answer, by hand ────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; A rule's conclusion of `neq` is checked, never stored (Q-M1e2.26). At root
;;; the rule concludes `(neq a a)`, which is false, so root is a contradiction,
;;; explained as `(false)` is: its core is `(p a)`, the premise the firing
;;; matched, and `refuted by` names `r`. R is empty.
;;;
;;; ── What the engine answered ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The conclusion was stored: root held `(neq a a :rule r)` (`ein saturate
;;; FILE --dump`), H was empty and R {∅}, exit 0 (`ein solve FILE` and `ein
;;; oracle FILE` at `ec004a48`, the engine `916d97ac`'s, release and debug
;;; alike). Root's state file did not load: a fact line `(neq a a)` is a
;;; parse error. The engine now gives the answer by hand.
;;;
;;;
;;;

(relation p Node :why "p holds of {?1}")
(p a)
(rule r () :match (p ?x) :assert (neq ?x ?x) :why "p holds of {?x}, so {?x} differs from itself, which cannot be")
